WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:25.980
COMPUTER VIRUSES:
Remember 10 to 20 years ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:31.920
when we were all scared of getting a computer&nbsp;
virus? We would pay for antivirus protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.820
from companies like McAfee and Norton just so&nbsp;
that we wouldn’t get a “Trojan Virus”. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:45.420
somehow, despite all our efforts, getting a&nbsp;
virus was often an inevitability. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.440
we’d have to boot our computers into safe&nbsp;
mode, delete recently downloaded files,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.280
run virus scans, and hope and pray&nbsp;
that the computer will actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:59.100
boot up the next time and that no data&nbsp;
was corrupted or lost. But, nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.560
the thought of a computer virus destroying&nbsp;
our computer rarely crosses our mind. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.440
many of us have completely stopped purchasing&nbsp;
external anti virus protection, yet our computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:16.800
experience is better than ever and we don’t even&nbsp;
have to deal with those slow virus scans. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:23.520
did computer viruses just get erradicated like&nbsp;
Polio or Small Pox? But wait, that can’t be true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.820
because we still hear about data breaches and&nbsp;
hacks all the time. So, clearly, computers still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.489
have a bunch of vulnerabilities. But then,&nbsp;
what happened to consumer computer viruses?

00:01:34.489 --> 00:01:35.340
HISTORY OF VIRUSES:&nbsp;

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.900
Before we answer why viruses are no longer a big&nbsp;
threat for most us, we have to understand how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:51.660
viruses work in the first place. Computer viruses&nbsp;
have been around for a long time with the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:57.240
one called “Creeper” dating back to 1971. The&nbsp;
mechanics of the virus were extremely simple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.460
Once the virus was placed inside a local hard&nbsp;
drive, it would just keep replicating itself until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:08.040
the given computer ran out of space and became&nbsp;
inoperational. At this point, the virus would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:13.620
taunt the user with the message “I'M THE CREEPER.&nbsp;
CATCH ME IF YOU CAN!” Fortunately, the Creeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:19.200
wasn’t hard to catch because it came along with&nbsp;
an antidote called Reaper. This is because Creeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.180
wasn’t actually designed with malicious intent. It&nbsp;
was simply supposed to demonstrate basic computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:29.820
vulnerabilities and how these shortfalls could be&nbsp;
addressed. But, the days of benign experimental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:35.580
viruses didn’t last long. As soon as computers&nbsp;
became generally available, a new subset of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:40.140
people called “Hackers” started trying to reverse&nbsp;
engineer code and we started seeing much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:47.280
deadly viruses. This included the first real world&nbsp;
virus called “Brain” in 1986. Unlike Creeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.020
Brain didn’t just aim to waste your hard drive&nbsp;
space. Brain was trying to corrupt your computer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:57.960
and it did this by latching onto your boot disk.&nbsp;
From there, Brain would move your real boot sector&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:03.720
to another location and mark it as “bad”. In&nbsp;
the meantime, it would overwrite your real boot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:09.120
sector with the virus making it impossible to boot&nbsp;
the computer. The next prominent computer virus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.060
“Michelangelo” appeared in 1991 and worked in&nbsp;
a very similar fashion but with a unique twist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:20.040
Instead of corrupting the computer as soon as&nbsp;
Michelangelo made contact, Michelangelo would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.980
wait until March 6th of every year. This way,&nbsp;
no one would really know how much the virus had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:32.040
spread. And then suddenly, on March 6th, the virus&nbsp;
would overwrite the first 100 sectors with zeros&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:37.920
and everyone would be screwed over at the same&nbsp;
time. This strategy was especially effective as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:44.460
the virus would end up infecting 20,000 computers.&nbsp;
But, the next virus was so much bigger that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.780
Michelangelo would look like a school project.&nbsp;
Speaking of school projects, this next virus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:54.720
CIH, was developed by a university student&nbsp;
and launched in 1998. The motive? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:00.540
the university student wanted to put overconfident&nbsp;
software engineers in their place. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:06.420
it’s safe to say that that goal was destroyed.&nbsp;
Once CIH made it to a user’s computer, it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:11.460
overwrite the hard drive, the boot sector,&nbsp;
and even the bios. Similar to Michelangelo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:16.860
CIH was designed to remain dormant for 1 month&nbsp;
before starting its rampage, but when the virus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:22.800
was triggered, it was absolutely deadly. CIH&nbsp;
would end up infecting a whopping 60 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:29.460
computers and causing $1 billion worth of damage.&nbsp;
For the first time, computer viruses weren’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:35.160
a nuisance. They were an actual international&nbsp;
threat, and this threat would only get bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:41.460
with the internet. Up until now, the main culprit&nbsp;
spreading viruses were shared floppy drives, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:47.820
let’s just say the internet was much more viral.&nbsp;
The first prominent internet virus came in 1999&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:53.700
and was called Melissa. Melissa was distributed&nbsp;
through email and once you received Melissa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.980
it would automatically be sent to 50 of your&nbsp;
contacts. Fortunately, Melissa wasn’t malicious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:04.500
some would even call it a social service as&nbsp;
Melissa was simply distributing passwords to adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:12.000
websites. But, it’s successor “iloveyou” in 2000&nbsp;
wasn’t nearly as kind. On the surface, iloveyou&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:17.820
was a simple text file that was spread through&nbsp;
email. But, behind the scenes, it was actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:24.180
Visual Basic Script or a VBS that could overwrite&nbsp;
office, image, and audio files. And thanks to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:29.760
virality of the internet, it would spread to&nbsp;
50 million computers in less than 10 days. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:36.000
marked the beginning of the peak computer virus&nbsp;
era. But, within the next 10 to 15 years, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.580
fear would disappear as if it never existed in&nbsp;
the first place. So, what in the world happened?

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:45.480
ERADICATION:&nbsp;

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:51.000
Well, as you saw with all those viruses, there’s&nbsp;
only two ways that they can spread: shared storage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:57.240
devices and the internet. The first medium was&nbsp;
naturally eliminated thanks to the internet. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:02.820
that people could email each other, there was less&nbsp;
of a need to share floppy disks or USB sticks. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:07.500
you were gonna use an external storage device, it&nbsp;
was more than likely just for personal use. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:13.080
the storage device medium kind of died out on&nbsp;
it’s own, but the internet medium was much harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:18.780
to address. A lot of this process simply consisted&nbsp;
of small improvements to security over a long time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:25.080
period. But there was a pinpointable event that&nbsp;
leaped forward internet security and that was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:32.580
ditching of Internet Explorer. Back in 2004, the&nbsp;
market share of Internet Explorer peaked at 95%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:39.600
Most of you probably remember IE as the slow,&nbsp;
buggy, crappy browser, but it was much worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:45.240
than just that as it also had serious security&nbsp;
vulnerabilities. You see, IE didn’t just allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:51.180
for the execution of Javascript which is the&nbsp;
standard for websites. IE also allowed for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:56.760
execution of VB scripts which gave the browser&nbsp;
access to the computer. This probably sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:02.940
like a stupid idea, but there was reason for it.&nbsp;
Ideally, this would allow websites to accomplish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:08.700
much more advanced tasks as they had access to&nbsp;
the computer. But, this also meant that they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:14.700
accomplish much more nefarious tasks, and that’s&nbsp;
exactly what happened. Microsoft didn’t really do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:20.220
much about this other than putting out a tutorial&nbsp;
on how to disable VBScript execution. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:25.860
let’s be honest, this was not something that the&nbsp;
average user was ever going to do. Something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:31.140
the average user did do though was ditch Internet&nbsp;
Explorer and move to Chrome. And unlike Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:37.200
Google didn’t support VBScript which made these&nbsp;
viruses virtually useless. By this point, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:42.240
storage device medium and the direct from browser&nbsp;
medium were effectively cut off which left us with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.100
just the downloading medium. The eradication&nbsp;
of this medium came down to multiple factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:54.720
starting with constant updates. Before 2010, there&nbsp;
was really no such thing as regular updates. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.740
got a version of Windows when you bought your&nbsp;
PC, and you would have this version up until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:05.580
you got another PC. But, moving into the 2010s,&nbsp;
constant over the air updates became a thing. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:11.400
while these updates somehow always happened at&nbsp;
the worst times possible, they did wonders for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:17.400
computer security. Viruses no longer had years on&nbsp;
end to keep exploiting the same bugs and glitches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:23.460
As soon as a virus was discovered, companies were&nbsp;
able to patch it and send out an update. We should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:28.200
also mention that companies started taking&nbsp;
cybersecurity much more seriously. Between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:35.220
2010 and 2018 alone, cybersecurity spending&nbsp;
rocketed from $27.4 billion to $66 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:40.620
These companies built out entire cybersecurity&nbsp;
departments filled with extraordinarily talented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:47.280
and well paid engineers. At Microsoft, for&nbsp;
example, security analysts are paid $272K on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.260
average. Speaking of compensation, companies&nbsp;
also starting disuading hackers by straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:58.020
up bribing them. Google for instance, offers&nbsp;
$1 million if you can hack Chrome. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.360
Apple is offering a million and a half if you can&nbsp;
hack the iPhone. And these companies end up paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:08.340
out several people every single year, and in&nbsp;
return, they’re able to prevent exploits that may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:13.560
have harmed hundreds of millions of users. In the&nbsp;
meantime, users have also gotten much more tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:19.020
savvy when it comes to protecting their devices.&nbsp;
We should also mention that our internet usage has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:25.020
also become much more focused. While we all spend&nbsp;
more time than ever on the internet, virtually all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:29.940
of our usage has to do with reputable companies&nbsp;
and brands like Google, Facebook, Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:36.000
Apple, Amazon, etc. So, the chances we’re even&nbsp;
exposed to a virus is significantly lower than 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.820
to 20 years ago. And when you put all of these&nbsp;
factors together, you get the virus free era.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:48.300
SHIFTING MOTIVES:
None of this is to say that computer viruses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:53.220
are completely gone though. The number of bad&nbsp;
actors and threats are actually larger than ever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:59.400
And one of the main reasons that we don’t see&nbsp;
viruses affect us is because bad actors no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:05.340
target everyday users. Back in the day, the only&nbsp;
incentive of creating a virus was to make a splash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:12.000
in the tech scene and/or expose vulnerabilities.&nbsp;
But, nowadays, hacking is a lot more profitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:17.220
and I’m not talking about selling your hacks&nbsp;
to big names companies. I’m talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:22.920
using exploits to steal loads of data and operate&nbsp;
international cyber crime rings that do everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:29.100
from creating fake IDs to credit card fraud. And&nbsp;
this makes perfect sense. Let’s say you found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:34.800
a data exploit in Windows after years of work.&nbsp;
Would you rather try to spread this virus amongst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:41.040
everyday users and display some corny gotcha&nbsp;
message or would you rather target an institution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:48.060
I mean the answer is obvious. Stealing the data of&nbsp;
100 million users from T-Mobile is infinitely more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:53.340
valuable than corrupting random people’s PCs.&nbsp;
This information is literally worth billions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:57.960
Given that you’ll have to sell it through dark&nbsp;
avenues, you’ll probably have to accept a deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:03.480
discount and sell it for hundreds of millions,&nbsp;
but that’s still 100 times what Google is willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:09.720
to pay you for that exploit. Also, even if you&nbsp;
were going for everyday people, the goal would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:15.720
not be to screw up their machine. In fact, the&nbsp;
goal would be exact opposite as you wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:21.300
want the user to even notice. Ideally, the virus&nbsp;
should be able to collect data permanently and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:26.580
not inhibit any functionality whatsoever. If you&nbsp;
do this at scale, you can have constant streams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:31.740
of data from tens of millions if not hundreds of&nbsp;
millions of people. And thanks to cryptocurrency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:37.200
hackers are able to transact insane amounts of&nbsp;
money with ease. This one hacker, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:43.080
claims that he’s owns $7 billion worth of&nbsp;
Bitcoin making him the 369th richest person in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:49.200
the world. So, while viruses no longer affect us&nbsp;
directly, they’re very much still a major threat.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.920
THE STATE OF VIRUSES:&nbsp;

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:59.040
In the end, we all remember computer viruses as a&nbsp;
symbol of computers throughout the 2000s. Thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:04.500
to much safer browsers, constant updates, large&nbsp;
security departments, and much more savvy users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:09.480
traditional computer viruses have become a&nbsp;
thing of the past. This isn’t to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:14.280
cybersecurity threats have been completely&nbsp;
eliminated altogether. Rather, they’ve simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:19.680
shifted in their form and their motive. Viruses,&nbsp;
today, don’t aim to slow down your computer or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:26.160
corrupt it. They aim to steal your data and sell&nbsp;
it for millions. And judging by the constant data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:32.160
leaks that we see on a regular basis, it looks&nbsp;
like there’s still a long way to go. But hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:38.100
at least we don’t have to deal with Trojan viruses&nbsp;
and safe mode anymore. When was the last time you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:43.320
had a computer virus? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you’re glad that we’re past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:47.640
traditional viruses. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:51.420
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

